\section{Time formatting utilities}

Canvas states its timestamps as instants---ISO~8601 strings in UTC---while a
reader wants the reading a clock on their own wall showed at that moment.
[[format_local_time]] performs that conversion for everything the CLI
displays: submission times, calendar events, assignment deadlines.

Turning an instant into a wall-clock reading requires a UTC offset, and
choosing which offset is the same question \cref{sec:utils:local-zone} works
through for the opposite direction, where a wall-clock reading had to become
an instant. This function is the display-direction twin of that
parse-direction bug, down to the spelling: [[arrow]]'s [[to('local')]]
resolves the word by evaluating [[datetime.now().astimezone().tzinfo]] once,
at the moment of the call, so what reaches [[to()]] is a frozen offset rather
than the local zone. Reading a December deadline from an August terminal in
Stockholm therefore rendered it in CEST: [[2023-12-15T13:30:00Z]] was
displayed as [[15:30]], an hour past the [[14:30]] that Stockholm clocks
actually read that afternoon.

We therefore hand [[to()]] the zone itself, from [[local_timezone()]], and
let it resolve the offset against the instant being displayed rather than
against the instant of the call.

<<functions>>=
def format_local_time(iso_ts):
  """
  Formats an ISO timestamp string in local time (YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm).

  The offset applied is the one in force at that timestamp's own instant,
  not the one in force when the conversion runs.
  """
  if not iso_ts:
    return "N/A"
  try:
    return arrow.get(iso_ts).to(local_timezone()).format('YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm')
  except Exception:
    return iso_ts
@

\subsection{Verifying [[format_local_time]]}

Now let's verify this function handles edge cases and formats timestamps correctly.
The [[format_local_time()]] function is used to display timestamps to users.

What these tests need pinned is \emph{where} they run, not \emph{when}: the
input names its own instant, so the expected reading follows from the zone
alone, and the fixture above supplies that. The conversion test used to
freeze the clock as well, on the date of its own input---which is precisely
the arrangement under which a sampled offset and a resolved one agree, so
the freeze could only hide the defect above. We drop it and let the test say
what it means: this answer does not depend on when it is computed.

<<test functions>>=
class TestFormatLocalTime:
    """Test format_local_time() with various inputs"""

    def test_none_returns_na(self):
        """None input should return 'N/A' for display"""
        assert format_local_time(None) == "N/A"

    def test_empty_string_returns_na(self):
        """Empty string should return 'N/A'"""
        assert format_local_time("") == "N/A"

    def test_formats_utc_to_local(self):
        """
        Should convert UTC timestamp to local time.

        Input: 2023-12-15 13:30:00 UTC
        Expected output in local time (CET, UTC+1 in Stockholm): 14:30
        """
        utc_time = "2023-12-15T13:30:00Z"
        result = format_local_time(utc_time)
        assert result == "2023-12-15 14:30"

    def test_invalid_format_returns_original(self):
        """If parsing fails, return the original string"""
        invalid = "not-a-timestamp"
        result = format_local_time(invalid)
        assert result == invalid
@

\subsection{Verifying the displayed offset follows the instant}

The conversion test above still cannot tell a zone from an offset on its
own. It asks about a single instant, and a single instant is answered
correctly by a sampled offset for half of every year---run it in December
and CET is the right answer for the wrong reason. Only a second instant on
the other side of a transition removes that escape, since no one offset
satisfies both.

The two tests below make that pairing the invariant and vary only how far
apart the paired instants lie. The first takes them six months apart, where
the two readings differ. The second is the sharper statement: [[2023-10-29]]
is the morning Sweden turns its clocks back, and the pair sits an hour apart
on that one date, so no offset chosen per run---or even per date---can
produce both answers. It also shows what a zone does that an offset cannot,
by holding the reading invariant while the instant varies: [[02:30]] happens
twice that morning, once in CEST and once in CET.

<<test functions>>=
class TestFormatLocalTimeAcrossDST:
    """The displayed offset must come from the instant, not from today"""

    def test_summer_and_winter_instants_read_differently(self):
        """
        Stockholm reads 13:30 UTC as 15:30 in July and as 14:30 in December.

        A single offset sampled at 'now' cannot satisfy both assertions.
        """
        summer = format_local_time("2023-07-15T13:30:00Z")
        winter = format_local_time("2023-12-15T13:30:00Z")
        assert summer == "2023-07-15 15:30"
        assert winter == "2023-12-15 14:30"

    def test_instants_an_hour_apart_straddle_the_transition(self):
        """
        Sweden turns its clocks back at 01:00 UTC on 2023-10-29.

        Both instants read 02:30 locally: the first in CEST, the second CET.
        """
        before = format_local_time("2023-10-29T00:30:00Z")
        after = format_local_time("2023-10-29T01:30:00Z")
        assert before == "2023-10-29 02:30"
        assert after == "2023-10-29 02:30"
@

Canvas expects every timestamp as an ISO~8601 string in UTC ending with
\enquote{Z}. The values we must convert reach us from several directions:
YAML front matter yields [[datetime.date]] and [[datetime.datetime]] objects,
our own date parsing yields [[arrow.Arrow]] objects, and both users and Canvas
hand us strings. So [[format_canvas_time]] dispatches on the type of its
argument and normalizes all four representations to that one output format.

A falsy argument---[[None]] or the empty string---means \enquote{no date was
given}, and we answer with [[None]] so that callers can leave the field out of
the request they are building.

Because [[None]] already carries that meaning, the type dispatch must be
\emph{total}. Any value that falls off the end of the [[if]]-chain makes Python
return [[None]] implicitly, and the caller cannot tell that answer apart from
\enquote{no date was given}. This is not a hypothetical: YAML's [[safe_load]]
resolves scalar types on its own, and it resolves several date-like things to
integers. The front matter line [[due_at: 20260306]] becomes the integer
20260306, and [[due_at: 19:00]] becomes 1140, because YAML~1.1 reads a
colon-separated scalar as a sexagesimal number. Both look like dates to whoever
wrote the front matter; both arrive here as [[int]].

The contrast between the two possible answers is what makes this worth getting
right. A silent [[None]] propagates: [[assignments edit]] stores it in the
update it builds, [[canvasapi]] passes it along, and [[requests]] omits
null-valued fields when it encodes the request body---so the date never reaches
Canvas at all. Canvas answers success, the command logs the assignment as
updated, and the deadline the user meant to change is still the old one. A loud
[[ValueError]] instead names the type of the offending value, which is exactly
what the user needs in order to fix the front matter.

We therefore close the chain with an [[else]] branch that rejects everything
else. The branch itself raises [[TypeError]]---in Python, a value of the
wrong \emph{type} is a [[TypeError]], while a value of the right type in
the wrong \emph{shape} is a [[ValueError]]---but the caller never sees
that distinction: the surrounding handler catches it and re-raises as the
same [[ValueError]] that an unparseable string produces, since to the
\emph{user} both are one mistake, a date Canvas cannot receive.
We place the [[raise]] inside the [[try]], as the chain's final branch,
for two reasons. It keeps the dispatch
visibly complete at exactly the spot where a maintainer would add a fifth
accepted type. And the surrounding handler wraps it, so it reads
\enquote{Invalid date/time format: Unsupported date/time type: int}: every
rejection from this function then shares one prefix. Raising after the [[try]]
would yield a slightly shorter message, but at the price of two message shapes
for callers to recognize---and some callers only forward what they catch, like
[[parse_date_attribute]] in the [[discussions]] module, which logs it as a
warning.
<<functions>>=
def format_canvas_time(iso_ts):
  """
  Converts a timestamp to the UTC ISO format Canvas expects, ending with 'Z'.

  Accepts datetime, date, arrow.Arrow and str values; falsy values give None.
  A value that names no timezone of its own is read as local time, using the
  UTC offset that applied on that value's own date.
  Raises ValueError for unparseable strings and for unsupported types.
  """
  if not iso_ts:
    return None
  try:
    if isinstance(iso_ts, datetime.datetime):
      <<read a naive [[datetime]] in the local zone>>
    elif isinstance(iso_ts, datetime.date):
      <<read a [[date]] as the local start of its day>>
    elif isinstance(iso_ts, arrow.Arrow):
      return iso_ts.to('utc').isoformat().replace("+00:00", "Z")
    elif isinstance(iso_ts, str):
      <<read a string, keeping any offset it already carries>>
    else:
      raise TypeError(
        f"Unsupported date/time type: {type(iso_ts).__name__}")
  except Exception as e:
    raise ValueError(f"Invalid date/time format: {e}")
@

\subsection{Local time is a zone, not an offset}
\label{sec:utils:local-zone}

Three of the four branches are handed a wall-clock reading with no statement
of which clock it was read from: a naive [[datetime]], a bare [[date]], and a
string like [[2023-07-15T10:00:00]]. Our rule for all three is that the user
means their own local time. Turning such a reading into an instant requires a
UTC offset, and choosing \emph{which} offset is where this function used to go
wrong.

The tempting answer is to ask what the offset is right now, which is what the
code did:
\begin{quote}
[[dt.replace(tzinfo=arrow.now().tzinfo)]]
\end{quote}
That expression does not hand back the local \emph{zone}. It hands back a
[[datetime.timezone]]---a single frozen number together with the abbreviation
that happened to be current, such as
[[timezone(timedelta(seconds=7200), 'CEST')]]. Stamping it onto a naive value
asserts that the target instant lies on the same side of every daylight-saving
transition as today does.

For roughly half of all date pairs, it does not. Setting a December deadline
from a July terminal in Stockholm applied CEST ([[+02:00]]) to a date on which
CET ([[+01:00]]) was actually in force, so [[assignments set-dates]] pushed the
deadline an hour early; doing it the other way round pushed it an hour late.
The error is invisible in the output---a plausible timestamp, just not the one
the user asked for---and it only appears when the two dates straddle a
transition, which is why the original tests never caught it: [[freeze_time]]
put \enquote{now} on the same date as the input.

Arrow's own shorthand, [[tzinfo='local']], looks like the cure and is the same
disease. Its parser resolves the word by evaluating
[[datetime.now().astimezone().tzinfo]]---again a fixed offset sampled at the
moment of the call, not a zone.

What the naive branches need is the zone itself: an object that is
\emph{asked} for an offset and answers per instant, consulting the transition
rules rather than reciting a stored number. [[dateutil]]'s [[tzlocal()]] is
exactly that. We wrap it in a function rather than a module-level constant
because it samples the process's zone configuration when it is constructed, so
a constant would freeze the zone at import time---and the tests below change
[[TZ]] while the module is already loaded.

<<functions>>=
def local_timezone():
  """
  Returns the local timezone as a zone rather than as a fixed UTC offset.

  The result resolves its offset against the datetime it is asked about, so a
  timestamp gets the offset that applied on its own date rather than the one
  that happens to apply today.
  """
  return dateutil.tz.tzlocal()
@

[[dateutil]] is absent from our declared dependencies, yet it is not an
optional extra: [[arrow]] requires it and imports it itself, so it is
available wherever this module can be imported at all.

<<imports>>=
import dateutil.tz
@

With the zone in hand, the two object branches read alike. A [[datetime]] that
already carries a timezone is left as it is---its caller has answered the
question we would otherwise be guessing at, and \cref{sec:utils:explicit-offset}
extends that same courtesy to strings.

<<read a naive [[datetime]] in the local zone>>=
dt = iso_ts
if dt.tzinfo is None:
  dt = dt.replace(tzinfo=local_timezone())
return dt.astimezone(datetime.timezone.utc).isoformat().replace("+00:00", "Z")
@

A [[date]] names no time of day at all, so we read it as the moment its day
begins locally---the reading a user means by \enquote{due on the 15th}.

<<read a [[date]] as the local start of its day>>=
dt = datetime.datetime.combine(iso_ts, datetime.time(0, 0))
dt = dt.replace(tzinfo=local_timezone())
return dt.astimezone(datetime.timezone.utc).isoformat().replace("+00:00", "Z")
@

\subsection{Strings that already name their zone}
\label{sec:utils:explicit-offset}

Strings are the one input type that may answer the timezone question for
itself. [[2023-12-15T10:00:00]] leaves it open, but [[2023-12-15T13:30:00Z]]
settles it, and so does anything ending in an offset such as [[+00:00]].

The old code applied the local zone to every string alike. For a string that
carried an offset, that did not \emph{convert} the value---it kept the digits
and discarded the offset, which silently moves the instant. In CET,
[[2023-12-15 13:30:00+00:00]] came back as [[2023-12-15T11:30:00Z]]: the same
wall-clock reading, reattributed to the wrong zone and then converted out of
it, so the result was off by the reader's own UTC offset.

The consequence was a command whose answer depended on where it was run:
[[calendar list]] normalizes [[--start-time]] and [[--end-time]] through
[[arrow]] first, which renders them with an explicit [[+00:00]], and then
hands those strings here---so the range Canvas was queried for was shifted by
the local offset, and events near either end of it were included or dropped
accordingly.

The distinction we need already exists in this module.
[[_has_explicit_timezone]] (\cref{sec:utils:timezone-detection}) was written
for [[parse_date]], which faces the identical question one layer up, and
asking it here makes the two entry points agree instead of contradicting each
other. We apply the local zone only when the string leaves the question open,
and otherwise let [[arrow]] read the offset the string states.

<<read a string, keeping any offset it already carries>>=
if _has_explicit_timezone(iso_ts):
  instant = arrow.get(iso_ts)
else:
  instant = arrow.get(iso_ts, tzinfo=local_timezone())
return instant.to('utc').isoformat().replace("+00:00", "Z")
@

\subsection{Verifying [[format_canvas_time]]}

Now let's verify this more complex function handles multiple input types correctly.
The [[format_canvas_time()]] function accepts datetime objects, arrow objects, date objects, and strings, converting them all to Canvas's expected UTC format ending with 'Z'.

This is a perfect example of variation theory in action---we test the same conversion logic with many different input types, maintaining the invariant: always produce Canvas-compatible UTC timestamps ending with 'Z'.

The last two tests vary the input in the opposite direction: a string that is
not a date, and a value whose type is not a date at all. Here the invariant is
that both are rejected the same way, with a [[ValueError]] carrying the same
message prefix. The type test is the regression test for the silent [[None]]
described above; asserting on the message keeps the two failure modes from
drifting apart again.

