\subsection{Verifying the offset comes from the target date}

Every test above freezes \enquote{now} on the same date as its input, so none
of them can distinguish an offset resolved at the input's own date from one
sampled at \enquote{now}: on those inputs the two agree. Freezing does more
damage than that, though. Under [[freezegun]] the very expression the old code
relied on, [[arrow.now().tzinfo]], evaluates to a genuine zone object rather
than to the frozen offset it yields in a live process---so a frozen test could
not have caught this bug however far apart the two dates were placed. The
tests were not merely pointed away from the defect; the harness had disarmed
it.

These tests therefore do not freeze the clock at all. That is not an
oversight but the property under test: the answer must not depend on when it
is computed. Each test pairs a summer expectation with a winter one, and no
single offset satisfies both---so whichever season the machine's calendar
happens to be in, one of the two assertions catches an offset that was
sampled rather than resolved. The three tests vary only the input type,
keeping that pairing invariant, which is what makes them read as one rule
applied three times rather than three separate facts.

