\subsection{Verifying that a stated offset survives}

The rule from \cref{sec:utils:explicit-offset} shows up best against its own
opposite, so these tests hold the \emph{instant} invariant and vary only how
it is spelled. All four inputs below name one and the same moment: once with
a [[Z]], once with an offset far from any the local zone ever uses, once in
the [[+00:00]] spelling that [[calendar list]] produces, and finally with no
offset at all, where the local zone legitimately supplies the answer. A
string that is reinterpreted rather than converted keeps its digits, so it
lands on a different instant, and only the last of the four still agrees.

We choose [[-05:00]] rather than a nearby offset on purpose: Stockholm is
[[+01:00]] or [[+02:00]], never [[-05:00]], so the assertion holds regardless
of which side of the year the test happens to run on.

