\section{Timezone detection utilities}
\label{sec:utils:timezone-detection}

Two entry points in this module have to settle the same question about a
string before they can turn it into an instant: does the string already say
which clock its wall-clock reading was taken from?
[[format_canvas_time]] (\cref{sec:utils:explicit-offset}) asks it of the
values a command is about to send to Canvas, and [[parse_date]] asks it of
whatever the user typed. Both act on the answer identically---apply the local
zone when the string leaves the question open, stand back when it does
not---so a wrong answer costs the same in either place.

What it costs is an instant that quietly moves, and it moves in both
directions:

\begin{description}
\item[Calling a naive string explicit] withholds the local zone, so [[arrow]]
  falls back on its own default and reads the digits as UTC. A December
  deadline typed in Stockholm is then stored an hour late.
\item[Calling an explicit string naive] hands it to
  [[arrow.get(s, tzinfo=local_timezone())]], and that argument does not
  \emph{convert} a stated offset---it \emph{overrides} it. In CET,
  [[20231215T083000-0500]] came back as [[2023-12-15T07:30:00Z]] instead of
  [[2023-12-15T13:30:00Z]], six hours off.
\end{description}

Either way the output is a well-formed timestamp that no caller can tell
apart from a right one, which is what makes the detector worth the care that
follows.

\subsection{The spellings the detector must judge}

Four producers feed strings into those two entry points, and between them
they fix what the answer has to be right about:

\begin{description}
\item[Canvas] answers in ISO~8601 UTC with a trailing [[Z]], as in
  [[2023-12-15T13:30:00Z]]; the [[discussions]] module sends such values back
  through [[format_canvas_time]].
\item[ [[calendar list]] ] normalizes [[--start-time]] and [[--end-time]]
  through [[arrow]] before passing them on, and [[arrow]]'s default rendering
  is [[YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm:ssZZ]]: a space where ISO~8601 writes [[T]], and the
  offset spelled [[+00:00]].
\item[YAML front matter] carries whatever an assignment author wrote, which
  may or may not name a zone.
\item[The command line] carries free text, the widest source of the four:
  anything a user might reasonably type after [[--due-at]].
\end{description}

A fifth constraint sits underneath those. Whatever we report as explicit is
handed on to [[arrow.get]] with no zone argument, so the answers that
\emph{do} anything are the ones [[arrow]] can read back: a trailing [[Z]], or
an offset written [[+HH:MM]], [[+HHMM]] or [[+HH]], following either an
extended time ([[13:30]], [[13:30:00]]) or a basic one ([[1330]],
[[133000]]).

\subsection{Why substring tests cannot answer this}

The first implementation looked for those markers anywhere in the string:

\begin{quote}
[['Z' in date_str]] \quad or \quad [[date_str.count('-') > 2]]
\end{quote}

Neither test is anchored, so both answer a different question from the one
asked. [[Z]] is an ordinary letter as well as a zone marker, and
[[2023-12-15 10:00 NZST]] was therefore reported as stating UTC on the
strength of the [[Z]] in the middle of a zone abbreviation---as was any input
containing the letter at all, down to [[not-a-valid-date-xyz123]].

The hyphen count is a proxy, and it misses in both directions at once. It
over-fires because a hyphen is also a date separator, so a third one need not
mean an offset. It under-fires because it assumes the date spends exactly two
of them, which [[20231215T083000-0500]] and [[2023/12/15 08:30:00-05:00]] do
not---each states an offset the count cannot see, and each is the six-hour
error above.

\subsection{An offset is a suffix on a time}

Anchoring the marker to the end of the string cures the first failure but not
the second, because the end of a plain date is indistinguishable from an
offset: [[2023-12-15]] ends in [[-15]], spelled exactly as a two-digit offset
like [[-05]] is. Length cannot separate them, and neither can the value:
[[-12]] is both a legal UTC offset and December.

What separates them is what the marker is attached to. An offset qualifies a
time of day and never a date, so we require a time immediately in front of it
and read the marker only there. That one rule disposes of the whole family at
once: the [[-15]] in [[2023-12-15]] follows [[12]], and [[12]] is not a time.

A time announces itself in two shapes. The extended shape carries a colon
([[13:30]], [[13:30:00]]), and the colon is proof by itself. The basic shape
is bare digits ([[0830]], [[083000]]), which proves nothing on its own---read
that way, [[2023-12]] would be the year 2023 at offset [[-12]], and [[arrow]]
parses that same string as December~2023 today. So we accept a basic time
only where ISO~8601 puts one: directly after the [[T]] or the space that ends
the date.

\subsection{What we decline to recognize}

Three spellings that do name a zone are deliberately left out, for one shared
reason: nothing downstream would act differently on a [[True]] answer.

\begin{description}
\item[Zone abbreviations] ([[13:30:00 UTC]], [[10:00 NZST]]) name a zone that
  [[arrow]] cannot read at all, so [[format_canvas_time]] rejects such a
  string whichever answer we give. In [[parse_date]] they fall through to
  [[dateutil]], which turns the abbreviations it knows---[[UTC]] among
  them---into an aware [[datetime]] that [[format_canvas_time]] then leaves
  alone, and hands back the ones it does not know, [[NZST]] included, as a
  naive [[datetime]] that our local-time rule governs.
\item[ A lowercase [[z]] ] is likewise beyond [[arrow]]'s parser, and
  likewise resolved by [[dateutil]] on the one path that reaches that far.
\item[An offset behind a space] ([[13:30:00 +00:00]]) is that story once
  more: it is [[arrow]]'s own rendering plus a single space, and [[arrow]]
  will not read it back.
\end{description}

We do accept one spelling that no reader here parses: an offset carrying
seconds, [[+01:12:12]]. Python's [[datetime.isoformat()]] emits exactly that
for zones with sub-minute historical offsets, and when both answers are
operationally identical---each ends in the same [[ValueError]]---we would
rather record the true one.

<<imports>>=
import re
@

Compiled once at import, the pattern reads as the rule above: a time, an
optional fractional part, a zone marker, end of string.

<<constants>>=
EXPLICIT_TIMEZONE_SUFFIX = re.compile(
  r"""
  (?: (?<=[T\ ]) \d{4} (?: \d{2} )?    # basic time, after the date
    | \d{2} : \d{2} (?: : \d{2} )?     # extended time, proved by its colon
  )
  (?: [.,] \d+ )?                      # fractional seconds
  (?: Z                                # UTC
    | [+-] \d{2} (?: :? \d{2} (?: :? \d{2} )? )?   # offset from UTC
  )
  $
  """,
  re.VERBOSE)
@

Values that are not strings answer [[False]]: they cannot state a zone in
their text, and the callers that pass them have already decided the question
by type.

<<functions>>=
def _has_explicit_timezone(date_str):
  """
  Report whether a date string states the timezone it was read in.

  True for a trailing 'Z' and for a trailing UTC offset such as +02:00,
  -0500 or -05, provided a time of day precedes it.  False for a string
  naming no zone, for a zone abbreviation such as NZST, and for any value
  that is not a string.
  """
  if not isinstance(date_str, str):
    return False
  return EXPLICIT_TIMEZONE_SUFFIX.search(date_str) is not None
@

