\section{Date parsing utilities}

Function to parse date strings in various human-readable formats and convert
them to Canvas-compatible UTC timestamps. This function supports multiple
common date formats and provides flexible parsing for user input.

The key challenge is timezone interpretation. Users expect naive datetime
strings like [[2023-12-15 14:30]] to be interpreted as local time. However,
strings with explicit timezone markers like [[2023-12-15T14:30:00Z]] should
preserve their timezone.

We use conditional timezone interpretation:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Check if the string contains explicit timezone markers
\item For naive strings, apply [[tzinfo=local_timezone()]] to interpret as
      local time
\item For explicit timezone strings, use default parsing to preserve the timezone
\item For format-specific parsing, always apply [[tzinfo=local_timezone()]]
      since those formats never include timezone information
\item For natural-language input, hand the naive [[datetime]] we get back to
      [[format_canvas_time]], which applies the very same local-time rule
\end{enumerate}

The local zone arrives here as [[local_timezone()]] rather than as arrow's
[[tzinfo='local']] shorthand, and for the reason given in
\cref{sec:utils:local-zone}: the shorthand resolves to whatever offset is in
force when the call is made, so it dated every parse by the calendar of the
machine rather than by the date being parsed. That was the more visible half
of the defect, because [[parse_date]] is what reads the user's command line:
in July, [[parse_date("2023-12-15")]] answered [[2023-12-14T22:00:00Z]], an
hour before the midnight it was asked for.

The two halves also have to agree with each other. Natural-language input
takes the [[dateutil]] route and is localized inside [[format_canvas_time]],
while [[2023-12-15]] is localized here---and once one side resolves the
offset at the target date and the other samples it at \enquote{now}, the same
day spelled two ways names two different instants.

Besides dates, the function accepts a request to have \emph{no} date:
the words [[none]] and [[clear]], or an empty value.
For those it returns the empty string, because that is how
\enquote{no date} is spelled on the wire.
The function's contract is to return what Canvas should receive, and
[[None]] cannot fulfil it: [[canvasapi]] flattens an update dictionary
into form fields, and [[requests]] \emph{drops} every field whose value
is [[None]]---that is its mechanism for leaving optional parameters
out---so a [[None]] date never reaches Canvas at all and the old date
stays.
An empty string survives the encoding ([[assignment%5Bdue_at%5D=]]) and
Canvas reads it as clearing the date.
Only the input [[None]] still maps to [[None]]: no input is not a
request to clear anything.

<<functions>>=
def parse_date(date_str):
  """Parse a date string using arrow with multiple format attempts

  Returns a Canvas-compatible UTC timestamp ending with 'Z', the empty
  string when date_str asks for the date to be cleared ('none',
  'clear' or empty), or None when date_str is None.
  """
  if date_str is None:
    return None
  if not date_str or date_str.lower() in ['none', 'clear']:
    return ''

  # Try common formats in order of preference
  formats = [
    None,  # ISO format (arrow's default)
    'YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm:ss',
    'YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm',
    'YYYY-MM-DD',
    'MMM DD YYYY',
    'MMMM DD, YYYY',
    'MM/DD/YYYY',
    'DD/MM/YYYY',
    'YYYY/MM/DD'
  ]

  for fmt in formats:
    try:
      if fmt is None:
        if _has_explicit_timezone(date_str):
          return format_canvas_time(arrow.get(date_str))
        else:
          return format_canvas_time(arrow.get(date_str,
                                              tzinfo=local_timezone()))
      else:
        return format_canvas_time(arrow.get(date_str, fmt,
                                            tzinfo=local_timezone()))
    except (arrow.parser.ParserError, ValueError):
      continue

  <<fall back to natural-language parsing>>

  raise ValueError(f"Could not parse date: '{date_str}'. "
                  "Try formats like: YYYY-MM-DD, YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM, or MM/DD/YYYY")
@

\subsection{Falling back to natural-language parsing}

The format list above is deliberately short, so it misses spellings that are
perfectly ordinary to a human: [[December 15 2023]] writes the month in full
but omits the comma that [[MMMM DD, YYYY]] demands, and none of the other
formats matches it either. Rather than reject such input, we let [[dateutil]]
make a final attempt---it recognizes far more phrasings than we would care to
enumerate ourselves.

What [[dateutil]] hands back is a plain [[datetime]] object, and unless the
input carried an explicit offset, that object is naive. Naive input is
precisely what our local-time rule governs, so the only remaining question is
which code path gets to interpret it---and the two candidates disagree:

\begin{description}
\item[Through [[arrow.get()]] ] Arrow reads a naive [[datetime]] as UTC.
  [[December 15 2023]] becomes [[2023-12-15T00:00:00Z]]: midnight in
  Greenwich, not midnight where the user is.
\item[Through [[format_canvas_time()]] ] Its [[datetime]] branch attaches the
  local zone first and converts afterwards. In CET the same input becomes
  [[2023-12-14T23:00:00Z]]---the very instant that the plain [[2023-12-15]]
  yields through the format list.
\end{description}

We therefore pass the naive [[datetime]] straight to [[format_canvas_time]].
Routing it through [[arrow.get()]] first looks like a harmless change of
type, since the same conversion runs either way, but it settles the timezone
question before [[format_canvas_time]] ever sees a naive object---and settles
it the wrong way. The consequence is not cosmetic: [[assignments set-dates]]
would push due dates to Canvas shifted by the user's UTC offset, so a
deadline written as [[December 15 2023]] would fall due at the wrong hour
while the same deadline written as [[2023-12-15]] would not.

<<fall back to natural-language parsing>>=
try:
  import dateutil.parser
  parsed_date = dateutil.parser.parse(date_str)
  return format_canvas_time(parsed_date)
except (ImportError, ValueError):
  pass
@

\subsection{Verifying [[parse_date]]}

Now let's verify this user-facing function handles multiple date formats correctly.
The [[parse_date()]] function accepts human-readable date strings in many formats and converts them to Canvas format.
This function is particularly important because it processes user input from the command line.

We test multiple formats to ensure flexibility for users, who can input dates in many common formats and have them all converted to the standard Canvas format ending with 'Z'.

<<test functions>>=
class TestParseDate:
    """Test parse_date() with various date formats"""

    def test_none_returns_none(self):
        """None should return None"""
        assert parse_date(None) == None

    def test_none_string_returns_empty(self):
        """String 'none' asks to clear: the Canvas encoding is ''"""
        assert parse_date("none") == ""
        assert parse_date("None") == ""

    def test_clear_returns_empty(self):
        """String 'clear' asks to clear: the Canvas encoding is ''"""
        assert parse_date("clear") == ""

    def test_empty_string_returns_empty(self):
        """Empty string asks to clear: it already is the encoding"""
        assert parse_date("") == ""

    @freeze_time("2023-12-15 10:00:00")
    def test_iso_format(self):
        """ISO format: YYYY-MM-DD"""
        result = parse_date("2023-12-15")
        assert result.endswith("Z")
        # Date interpreted as start of day local time (CET = UTC+1)
        # 2023-12-15 00:00 CET = 2023-12-14 23:00 UTC
        assert "2023-12-14T23:00:00Z" == result

    @freeze_time("2023-12-15 10:00:00")
    def test_iso_with_time(self):
        """ISO format with time: YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm"""
        result = parse_date("2023-12-15 14:30")
        assert result.endswith("Z")
        # Time interpreted as local time (CET = UTC+1)
        # 2023-12-15 14:30 CET = 2023-12-15 13:30 UTC
        assert "2023-12-15T13:30:00Z" == result

    @freeze_time("2023-12-15 10:00:00")
    def test_slash_format(self):
        """US format: MM/DD/YYYY"""
        result = parse_date("12/15/2023")
        assert result.endswith("Z")
        # Date interpreted as start of day local time (CET = UTC+1)
        # 2023-12-15 00:00 CET = 2023-12-14 23:00 UTC
        assert "2023-12-14T23:00:00Z" == result

    @freeze_time("2023-12-15 10:00:00")
    def test_month_name_format(self):
        """Month name format: Dec 15 2023"""
        result = parse_date("Dec 15 2023")
        assert result.endswith("Z")
        # Date interpreted as start of day local time (CET = UTC+1)
        # 2023-12-15 00:00 CET = 2023-12-14 23:00 UTC
        assert "2023-12-14T23:00:00Z" == result

    @freeze_time("2023-12-15 10:00:00")
    def test_natural_language_agrees_with_iso(self):
        """
        Natural language must denote the same instant as the ISO spelling.

        'December 15 2023' matches no format in the list (the full month name
        without a comma), so it reaches the dateutil fallback.  Taking that
        path must not change the meaning of the date: both spellings name
        midnight local time, which in CET (UTC+1) is 23:00 UTC the day before.
        """
        assert parse_date("December 15 2023") == parse_date("2023-12-15")
        assert parse_date("December 15 2023") == "2023-12-14T23:00:00Z"

    def test_invalid_format_raises_error(self):
        """Completely invalid string should raise ValueError"""
        with pytest.raises(ValueError, match="Could not parse date"):
            parse_date("not-a-valid-date-xyz123")
@

These tests verify that [[parse_date()]] provides a flexible interface, handling:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Timezone bugs}: Code works across timezones using freezegun
\item \textbf{User input variety}: Many common date formats are accepted
\item \textbf{Edge cases}: None values, empty strings, special keywords
\item \textbf{Canvas compatibility}: All outputs end with 'Z' (UTC indicator)
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Verifying that parsing does not depend on today}

Those tests all freeze \enquote{now} on the date they parse, so they cannot
separate an offset resolved at the parsed date from one sampled at
\enquote{now}. The tests below therefore run on the real clock, pairing a
summer expectation with a winter one exactly as in
\cref{sec:utils:local-zone}: no single offset satisfies both, so one of them
fails whenever the offset is sampled.

The last test is the one that matters most for maintenance. Both spellings of
the same December day must name the same instant, but they reach that answer
along different routes---[[2023-12-15]] is localized inside [[parse_date]],
[[December 15 2023]] inside [[format_canvas_time]] via the [[dateutil]]
fallback. If either route is changed back to a sampled offset while the other
is not, the two answers part company for half the year, and this assertion
says so without needing to know which route was broken.

<<test functions>>=
class TestParseDateAcrossDST:
    """Parsing must not date its answer by the calendar of the machine"""

    def test_iso_date_uses_the_offset_of_its_own_date(self):
        """
        Midnight on 15 July is 22:00 UTC; midnight on 15 December is 23:00.

        With the offset sampled at 'now', both answers become whichever of
        the two the current season dictates.
        """
        assert parse_date("2023-07-15") == "2023-07-14T22:00:00Z"
        assert parse_date("2023-12-15") == "2023-12-14T23:00:00Z"

    def test_time_of_day_uses_the_offset_of_its_own_date(self):
        """14:30 local is 12:30 UTC in July but 13:30 UTC in December"""
        assert parse_date("2023-07-15 14:30") == "2023-07-15T12:30:00Z"
        assert parse_date("2023-12-15 14:30") == "2023-12-15T13:30:00Z"

    def test_both_localizing_routes_stay_in_step(self):
        """
        The format list and the dateutil fallback must localize alike.

        'December 15 2023' matches no format, so it is localized inside
        format_canvas_time, while '2023-12-15' is localized in parse_date.
        """
        assert parse_date("December 15 2023") == parse_date("2023-12-15")
        assert parse_date("July 15 2023") == parse_date("2023-07-15")
@
